{
  "updated_at": "2026-10-23 09:05 JST",
  "total": 50,
  "toyota_summary": "The European Parliament's three largest political groups agreed today on stricter CO2 emissions standards that will require automakers to meet near-zero emissions by 2035. The deal locks in what was already expected policy, but the unified backing from conservative, socialist, and Green parties means the regulation will almost certainly pass the full Parliament and become law. This represents the most aggressive timeline for combustion engine phase-out among major markets.\n\nFor global automakers, this tightens the vise on product planning. Europe accounts for roughly a quarter of worldwide vehicle sales, and a 2035 deadline forces investment decisions now on battery supply chains, factory retooling, and EV model lineups that won't reach showrooms for years. Competitors who haven't already locked in EV production capacity will face compressed timelines and higher costs to comply.\n\nWatch whether other major markets—particularly China and the U.S.—follow with similar or faster timelines over the next 12 months.",
